[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Tree Shaking in Angular: Optimizing Your Application Size"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Tree shaking is a dead code elimination technique used in modern JavaScript bundlers like Webpack (which Angular CLI utilizes) to reduce the size of your application bundles. It works by statically analyzing the import and export statements in your code to determine which modules are actually used and then excluding the unused ones from the final bundle."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"How Tree Shaking Works"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The process involves several steps:"},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Static Analysis: The bundler analyzes your code to identify all import and export statements."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Dependency Graph Creation: A dependency graph is created, representing the relationships between modules."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Marking Used Modules: The bundler identifies which modules are actually used by tracing the entry points of your application."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Eliminating Unused Modules: Any modules that are not marked as used are considered dead code and are removed from the final bundle."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Benefits of Tree Shaking"},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Reduced Bundle Size: Smaller bundles lead to faster download times and improved application performance."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Improved Performance: Faster loading times result in a better user experience."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Better Code Organization: Encourages modular code, making it easier to maintain and test."},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Tree Shaking in Angular Projects"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"Angular CLI is configured to automatically perform tree shaking during the build process when using the production flag. This is achieved through Webpack and its Terser plugin."},{"order":15,"type":"code","text":"ng build --prod"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"The '--prod' flag enables various optimizations, including tree shaking, minification, and ahead-of-time (AOT) compilation."},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"Writing Tree-Shakeable Code"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"To maximize the effectiveness of tree shaking, it's important to write your code in a way that allows the bundler to easily identify unused modules. Here are some best practices:"},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use ES Modules (ESM): ESM uses static import and export statements, which are easier for bundlers to analyze compared to CommonJS or AMD."},{"order":20,"type":"code","text":"import { Component, OnInit } from '@angular/core';

export class MyComponent implements OnInit {
  ngOnInit(): void {
    // ...
  }
}"},{"order":21,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid Side Effects: Side effects are operations that modify something outside of the function's scope. They can prevent tree shaking because the bundler cannot safely remove a module if it might have side effects."},{"order":22,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Import Only What You Need: Instead of importing entire libraries, import only the specific modules or functions that you need."},{"order":23,"type":"code","text":"// Instead of:
import * as _ from 'lodash';

// Do this:
import { map } from 'lodash-es';"},{"order":24,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use Pure Functions: Pure functions always return the same output for the same input and have no side effects. They are easier to tree-shake because the bundler can safely remove them if they are not used."},{"order":25,"type":"subheading","text":"Verifying Tree Shaking"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"After building your application with the '--prod' flag, you can inspect the generated bundles to verify that tree shaking is working correctly. You can use tools like Webpack Bundle Analyzer to visualize the contents of your bundles and identify any unused modules that are still included."},{"order":27,"type":"paragraph","text":"To install Webpack Bundle Analyzer:"},{"order":28,"type":"code","text":"npm install webpack-bundle-analyzer --save-dev"},{"order":29,"type":"paragraph","text":"Then, modify your 'angular.json' file to include the analyzer:"},{"order":30,"type":"code","text":""configurations": {
  "production": {
    // ...
    "budgets": [
      {
        "type": "initial",
        "maximumWarning": "500kb",
        "maximumError": "1mb"
      },
      {
        "type": "anyComponentStyle",
        "maximumWarning": "2kb",
        "maximumError": "4kb"
      }
    ],
    "fileReplacements": [
      {
        "replace": "src/environments/environment.ts",
        "with": "src/environments/environment.prod.ts"
      }
    ],
    "optimization": true,
    "outputHashing": "all",
    "sourceMap": false,
    "extractCss": true,
    "namedChunks": false,
    "aot": true,
    "extractLicenses": true,
    "vendorChunk": false,
    "buildOptimizer": true,
    "showCircularDependencies": false,
    "statsJson": true
  }
}"},{"order":31,"type":"paragraph","text":"Run 'ng build --prod --stats-json' and then analyze the stats.json file using the webpack-bundle-analyzer."},{"order":32,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":33,"type":"paragraph","text":"Tree shaking is a powerful technique for optimizing Angular applications by reducing bundle sizes and improving performance. By following best practices for writing tree-shakeable code and verifying the results with bundle analysis tools, you can ensure that your applications are as efficient as possible."}]
